@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1295 = private unnamed_addr constant [755 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `collections.namedtuple` in stub files.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`typing.NamedTuple` is the \22typed version\22 of `collections.namedtuple`.\0A\0AInheriting from `typing.NamedTuple` creates a custom `tuple` subclass in\0Athe same way as using the `collections.namedtuple` factory function.\0AHowever, using `typing.NamedTuple` allows you to provide a type annotation\0Afor each field in the class. This means that type checkers will have more\0Ainformation to work with, and will be able to analyze your code more\0Aprecisely.\0A\0A## Example\0A```pyi\0Afrom collections import namedtuple\0A\0Aperson = namedtuple(\22Person\22, [\22name\22, \22age\22])\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```pyi\0Afrom typing import NamedTuple\0A\0Aclass Person(NamedTuple):\0A    name: str\0A    age: int\0A```\0A", align 1
