@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1540 = private unnamed_addr constant [652 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `__init__` methods that return values.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `__init__` method is the constructor for a given Python class,\0Aresponsible for initializing, rather than creating, new objects.\0A\0AThe `__init__` method has to return `None`. Returning any value from\0Aan `__init__` method will result in a runtime error.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aclass Example:\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        return []\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aclass Example:\0A    def __init__(self):\0A        self.value = []\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [CodeQL: `py-explicit-return-in-init`](https://codeql.github.com/codeql-query-help/python/py-explicit-return-in-init/)\0A", align 1
