@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1556 = private unnamed_addr constant [993 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `subprocess.run` that send `stdout` and `stderr` to a\0Apipe.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AAs of Python 3.7, `subprocess.run` has a `capture_output` keyword argument\0Athat can be set to `True` to capture `stdout` and `stderr` outputs. This is\0Aequivalent to setting `stdout` and `stderr` to `subprocess.PIPE`, but is\0Amore explicit and readable.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport subprocess\0A\0Asubprocess.run([\22foo\22], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport subprocess\0A\0Asubprocess.run([\22foo\22], capture_output=True)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe because replacing `stdout=subprocess.PIPE` and\0A`stderr=subprocess.PIPE` with `capture_output=True` may delete comments attached\0Ato the original arguments.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python 3.7 release notes](https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/3.7.html#subprocess)\0A- [Python documentation: `subprocess.run`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/subprocess.html#subprocess.run)\0A", align 1
