@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1575 = private unnamed_addr constant [2490 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for lambda expressions and function definitions that can be replaced with a function from\0Athe `operator` module.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `operator` module provides functions that implement the same functionality as the\0Acorresponding operators. For example, `operator.add` is often equivalent to `lambda x, y: x + y`.\0AUsing the functions from the `operator` module is more concise and communicates the intent of\0Athe code more clearly.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aimport functools\0A\0Anums = [1, 2, 3]\0Atotal = functools.reduce(lambda x, y: x + y, nums)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport functools\0Aimport operator\0A\0Anums = [1, 2, 3]\0Atotal = functools.reduce(operator.add, nums)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThe fix offered by this rule is always marked as unsafe. While the changes the fix would make\0Awould rarely break your code, there are two ways in which functions from the `operator` module\0Adiffer from user-defined functions. It would be non-trivial for Ruff to detect whether or not\0Athese differences would matter in a specific situation where Ruff is emitting a diagnostic for\0Athis rule.\0A\0AThe first difference is that `operator` functions cannot be called with keyword arguments, but\0Amost user-defined functions can. If an `add` function is defined as `add = lambda x, y: x + y`,\0Areplacing this function with `operator.add` will cause the later call to raise `TypeError` if\0Athe function is later called with keyword arguments, e.g. `add(x=1, y=2)`.\0A\0AThe second difference is that user-defined functions are [descriptors], but this is not true of\0Athe functions defined in the `operator` module. Practically speaking, this means that defining\0Aa function in a class body (either by using a `def` statement or assigning a `lambda` function\0Ato a variable) is a valid way of defining an instance method on that class; monkeypatching a\0Auser-defined function onto a class after the class has been created also has the same effect.\0AThe same is not true of an `operator` function: assigning an `operator` function to a variable\0Ain a class body or monkeypatching one onto a class will not create a valid instance method.\0ARuff will refrain from emitting diagnostics for this rule on function definitions in class\0Abodies; however, it does not currently have sophisticated enough type inference to avoid\0Aemitting this diagnostic if a user-defined function is being monkeypatched onto a class after\0Athe class has been constructed.\0A\0A[descriptors]: https://docs.python.org/3/howto/descriptor.html\0A", align 1
